    "rawResponseText": "Hotel franchise fees typically include an **initial fee** plus **ongoing fees** such as royalties, marketing/ad funds, reservation, and loyalty-program fees.[1][4] In the U.S. hotel market, ongoing franchise fees are often around **4% to 6% of gross room revenue**, with marketing and reservation fees commonly adding roughly **2%** more, though brand and segment matter.[2]\n\nA simple way to think about them is:\n\n- **Initial franchise fee**: paid when joining the brand; often a flat amount with a per-room component.[1][4]\n- **Royalty fee**: the core brand-use fee, usually charged monthly as a percentage of revenue.[1][4][6]\n- **Marketing fee**: funds brand-wide advertising and sales programs.[1][2][4]\n- **Reservation/loyalty fees**: cover the central booking system and rewards program.[1][4]\n\nExamples from current brand disclosures show how widely fees vary: Marriott International lists an **initial franchise application fee of $120,000** and franchise fees of **6% of gross room sales** plus **3% of gross food and beverage sales**.[6] A 2025 industry guide also reports application fees around **$49,000** for some brands, with royalties near **5%** and marketing around **2.1%** of gross room revenue.[9]\n\nIf you want, I can also give you:\n- a **sample fee calculation** for a specific hotel size, or\n- a **brand-by-brand comparison** of hotel franchise fees.",
